 "units": [
  {
   "id": 1,
   "ref": "595–599",
   "en": "'What am I saying? Where am I? What madness is warping my mind? Unhappy Dido — is it only now that his [crimes|14,15] touch you? This was the time to feel it, when you were handing him your throne. So much for his right hand, so much for his good faith — the man who, they say, carries his household gods with him, the man who carried his father, worn out with age, on his own shoulders!",
   "en_marked": "'[What|1] [am I saying|2]? [Where|4] [am I|5]? [What|6] [madness|8] [is warping|9] my [mind|7]? [Unhappy|10] [Dido|11] — {is it only|Added: the Latin simply has nunc. The translator brings out Dido's bitter self-reproach at realising too late.} [now|12] that {his|Added for clarity: facta impia means impious deeds, and the translator makes clear they are Aeneas's.} [crimes|14,15] [touch|16] [you|13]? [This was the time|17] [to feel it|18], [when|19] [you were handing|21] him your [throne|20]. [So much for|22] his [right hand|23], [so much for|22] his [good faith|24] — {the man|Added: quem refers back to the owner of the right hand, so the translator names him to make the relative clause clear.} [who|25], [they say|28], [carries|29] [his|27] [household gods|30] [with him|26], {the man|Added again to introduce the second relative clause beginning quem.} [who|31] [carried|32] his [father|36], [worn out|34] [with age|35], [on his own shoulders|33]!",
