  {
   "id": 4,
   "ref": "607–612",
   "en": "Sun, whose fires look down on every deed done on this earth; Juno, witness and go-between of these torments of mine; Hecate, howled to at midnight crossroads in every city; you avenging Furies, and gods of dying Dido — hear this, turn your power, as it deserves, upon these wrongs, and listen to my prayers.'",
   "en_marked": "[Sun|1], [whose|2] [fires|4] [look down on|7] [every|6] [deed|5] {done|Added to make the English natural: opera omnia terrarum is simply all the works of the earth.} on this [earth|3]; [Juno|14], [witness|13] [and|12] [go-between|10] of [these|9] [torments|11] {of mine|Added: harum curarum is simply these cares. The translator shows that Dido means her own suffering.}; [Hecate|16], [howled to|18] at [midnight|15] [crossroads|17] [in|19] {every|Not in the Latin. Added to bring out per urbes, throughout the cities.} [city|20]; you [avenging|23] [Furies|22], [and|24] [gods|25] of [dying|26] [Dido|27] — [hear|28] [this|29], [turn|32] your [power|33], [as it deserves|30], upon {these|Added: malis is simply wrongs. The translator makes clear Dido means the wrongs done to her.} [wrongs|31], [and|34] [listen to|36] [my|35] [prayers|37].'",
